# localclock — must the clock be global?
# ring N=24, Kc=0.60, dt=0.020, elapsed T=80.0
# LOCAL cells own a local time and a local STEP, so their tick
# counters genuinely diverge (that is the dilation).

== reference ==
SYNC  R=0.996150

== Q1/Q2. does the LOCK survive going local? ==
   K    R(local)   dR vs SYNC   tick spread (max-min tau)
   1    0.982793    -1.34e-02   20
   2    0.982923    -1.32e-02   30
   4    0.982928    -1.32e-02   46
   8    0.983192    -1.30e-02   61
  16    0.983471    -1.27e-02   94
  32    0.983460    -1.27e-02   171
  64    0.983554    -1.26e-02   303
1000000    0.996043    -1.08e-04   1065

reading: the lock is the physics. If R survives, a global
clock was never carrying it.

== Q3. IS A BYTE ENOUGH? counter width M vs skew bound K ==
wrapped comparison can only order ticks while K < M/2.
    M     K   K<M/2    R(local)   tick spread
    8     2   yes     0.982923   30
    8     6   NO      0.996043   1065
   16     4   yes     0.982928   46
   16    12   NO      0.996043   1065
   64    40   NO      0.996043   1065
  256    64   yes     0.983554   303
  256   200   NO      0.996043   1065

== Q4. DETERMINISM — the adoptability test ==
Same run, opposite scan direction. CANONICAL advances the cell
with the smallest (t_i, i); ARRIVAL advances whichever eligible
cell the scan meets first (what threads give you for free).
   K    canonical |fwd-rev|   arrival |fwd-rev|
   1   0.00e+00 (exact)   1.91e-02 (DIFFERS)
   2   0.00e+00 (exact)   3.15e-02 (DIFFERS)
   4   0.00e+00 (exact)   5.61e-02 (DIFFERS)
   8   0.00e+00 (exact)   1.03e-01 (DIFFERS)
  16   0.00e+00 (exact)   2.02e-01 (DIFFERS)
  64   0.00e+00 (exact)   3.70e+00 (DIFFERS)

reading: the ratchet compares byte-identical reruns. If
CANONICAL is exact and ARRIVAL is not, then local clocks are
adoptable but ONLY with an order defined by state, never by
which core arrived first.
